# =============================================================================
# sparsa — Latin sparse type word in an en file (expect parse error).
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Locale surfaces are sealed — the en spelling of the sparse tensor type is `sparse`, so the Latin `sparsa` is refused in an en file
#   • Parse error — `PARSE040:wrong_locale_type` names the word written and the en spelling to use instead
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • writing `sparsa<f32, [2, 3]>` in an en file — write `sparse<f32, [2, 3]>`
#
# See also: sparsa
# =============================================================================

# Latin sparse type word in an en file (expect parse error)
#
# WHY: en and la spellings never mix in one file; the en pack owns `sparse`.
#
# DELIBERATE: this exemplum keeps the Latin spelling (`sparsa`) on purpose, to
# pin the PARSE040 `wrong_locale_type` diagnostic for the sparse tensor type.

main {
    let sparsa<f32, [2, 3]> grid ← empty
    print grid.count_nonzero()
}
